TUnit uses `[Before(…)]` / `[After(…)]` attributes with a `HookType` enum to control scope. Here's a sketch for each:

## 1. Before every test in a class (instance-level)

```csharp
public class MyTests
{
    [Before(Test)]
    public Task ResetDatabase(TestContext context)
    {
        // runs before each [Test] in this class
        InMemoryDb.Reset();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [After(Test)]
    public Task Cleanup(TestContext context)
    {
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [Test]
    public async Task OrderIsCreated() { /* … */ }
}
```

## 2. Once before/after the class (class-level)

```csharp
public class MyTests
{
    private static HttpClient _client;

    [Before(Class)]
    public static Task OpenFixture(ClassHookContext context)
    {
        _client = new HttpClient();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [After(Class)]
    public static Task DisposeFixture(ClassHookContext context)
    {
        _client.Dispose();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [Test]
    public async Task Ping() { /* use _client */ }
}
```

## 3. Once before/after all tests in the assembly

```csharp
public class GlobalSetup
{
    private static WireMockServer _server;

    [Before(Assembly)]
    public static Task StartWireMock(AssemblyHookContext context)
    {
        _server = WireMockServer.Start();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [After(Assembly)]
    public static Task StopWireMock(AssemblyHookContext context)
    {
        _server.Stop();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

## 4. Once for the entire test session (cross-assembly)

```csharp
public class SessionSetup
{
    [Before(TestSession)]
    public static Task ApplyMigrations(TestSessionContext context)
    {
        Database.Migrate();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [After(TestSession)]
    public static Task TearDown(TestSessionContext context)
    {
        Database.Drop();
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

## Summary table

| Scope | Attribute | Method must be | Context parameter |
|-------|-----------|---------------|-------------------|
| Each test | `[Before(Test)]` / `[After(Test)]` | Instance | `TestContext` |
| Class | `[Before(Class)]` / `[After(Class)]` | `static` | `ClassHookContext` |
| Assembly | `[Before(Assembly)]` / `[After(Assembly)]` | `static` | `AssemblyHookContext` |
| Session | `[Before(TestSession)]` / `[After(TestSession)]` | `static` | `TestSessionContext` |

**Key points:**
- All hook methods return `Task` (async-first design).
- The enum values (`Test`, `Class`, `Assembly`, `TestSession`) come from `TUnit.Core.HookType`.
- Class/assembly/session hooks must be `static`; test-level hooks are instance methods.
- The class containing assembly or session hooks can live anywhere in the project — TUnit discovers them automatically.
